---
/**
 * /stores/ — what it costs to sell online, and who owns the customer afterwards.
 *
 * WHY IT IS TOP LEVEL, LIKE /getting-paid/ AND UNLIKE /websites/compare/. The
 * website comparison sits under /websites/ because that section sells the thing
 * it compares against. Nothing on this page is for sale: a business already on
 * Etsy still has to decide whether to stay, and burying this under the website
 * product would have scoped it to people already buying one.
 *
 * IT PRICES ONE TERM ON PURPOSE — what the PLATFORM takes, before any card
 * processor's cut. src/lib/store-cost.js says why at length: a total including
 * processing compares a bundle against a component, which is why every published
 * store comparison disagrees with every other one. The processing half is
 * /getting-paid/'s job and every card here points at it.
 *
 * THE HOURS ARE THE LARGEST COST AND ARE NEVER PRICED. Each card answers it in
 * words, the page prints the owner's own figure beside the money rather than
 * inside it, and store-cost.js refuses in one named sentence to convert it. That
 * is the same refusal the free check makes about payback (L-193).
 *
 * THE CATALOG TABLE IS THE FINDING THE SHEET TURNS ON. Which platform a
 * business is on decides whether the one thing I sell here can be built for them
 * at all. It is not a ranking and the page says so — a platform refusing it is
 * not a worse platform, it is one on which a particular thing cannot be built.
 *
 * EVERY CARD MUST KEEP ITS `doesBetter` ROW AND A GATE ENFORCES IT.
 * scripts/verify-stores.mjs fails if any card's text is missing from the
 * rendered page and refuses one under 100 characters. Nobody deletes that row on
 * purpose; it gets softened in a copy pass until it says nothing, and a
 * comparison with no losing rows is an advertisement wearing a table.
 *
 * NO FIGURE IS TYPED IN THIS FILE. Every fee comes out of the data module and
 * every total is computed by src/lib/store-cost.js, so a Node gate checks the
 * same arithmetic the page runs rather than a copy of it.
 *
 * THE TITLE IS SHORT BECAUSE THE LAYOUT APPENDS THE PRACTICE NAME, and
 * verify-seo.mjs measures the whole rendered string rather than the half typed
 * here. The first draft carried the customer-ownership clause as well and came
 * out at 77 characters, past what a search result shows — so that clause moved
 * into the description, where there is room for it and where it still reaches a
 * reader deciding whether to click.
 */
/* ONE DIRECTORY DEEPER SINCE 2026-09-20. This page was src/pages/stores.astro
   until D118 deleted it; restoring it under the Websites door moved every
   relative import out by a level, exactly as src/pages/websites/compare.astro
   already is. The build catches a missed one, which is why they are not worth
   a second thought — Astro refuses to resolve it rather than shipping a blank. */
import MarketingLayout from '../../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import WhatIBuild from '../../components/WhatIBuild.astro';
import { cards, rows, READ_ON, EXAMPLES, catalogAccess, VERDICTS } from '../../data/stores.js';
import { compareStores, annualSpread, hoursCost, usd, pct } from '../../lib/store-cost.js';
import { ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;

/* Computed once, here, so no arithmetic happens inside a template. */
const tables = EXAMPLES.map((example) => {
  const table = compareStores(cards, example);
  return { example, table, spread: annualSpread(table), hours: hoursCost(example) };
});

const canBuild = catalogAccess.filter((r) => r.verdict === 'yes');
const cannotBuild = catalogAccess.filter((r) => r.verdict === 'no');

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${origin}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Websites', item: `${origin}/websites/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Selling online', item: `${origin}/websites/selling-online/` },
    ],
  },
];
---
<MarketingLayout
  schema={schema}
  title="What it costs to sell online"
  description="Who owns the customer when the sale is done, and what each platform takes before it. Shopify, Etsy, WooCommerce, BigCommerce, Ecwid, Big Cartel, Square, TikTok and Instagram, read off their own pages."
  image="/og/websites.png"
>
  {/* ── Hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D113 CARRIED OFF THE HOME PAGE, 2026-09-13. This is the longest sheet on
      the site and it ran seven bands at 6xl, 3xl, 5xl, 6xl, 5xl, 5xl, 3xl on a
      single vertical rhythm, with one ground alternating underneath. Four of
      those seven widths are within half an inch of each other, so a reader
      scrolling past nine screens of tables and cards had one signal to navigate
      by and it was the ground.

      The column now walks feature, popout, content, feature, content, whatever
      WhatIBuild brings, popout; the ground walks canvas, canvas, surface,
      canvas, deep, surface, canvas; and the rhythm tightens at the short
      preamble and opens out under the catalog table. Every neighbor differs in
      at least two of ground, column and rhythm, and not one of them differs in
      the palette — a hue is the lightest signal on a page, which is the
      argument src/components/kit/industrial.js makes under WHY THREE AND NOT
      SEVEN.

      THE DEEP GROUND IS SPENT ON THE CATALOG TABLE, which this file's own
      header calls the finding the sheet turns on. It is the heaviest signal the
      site has and the one band here that decides whether the single thing sold
      on this page can be built for the reader at all — the money tables above
      it are the same arithmetic every sheet does, and the deep ground is not
      for the thing a sister page also has.

      ONE BAND ON THIS PAGE IS NOT THIS PAGE'S TO SET. WhatIBuild.astro carries
      its own section and its own surface ground, shared with /websites/compare/
      and /getting-paid/, so the bands on either side of it are chosen against a
      ground this file cannot change. */}
  <Section width="feature" class="border-b border-line">
    <Fragment slot="backdrop">
      <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    </Fragment>
      <p class="mono-label">Every option, read off their own pages on {READ_ON}</p>
      <h1 class="statement mt-3 max-w-4xl">
        The fee is the small question. <span class="aiq-accent">Who owns the customer is the large one.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Two stores can cost you the same money this month and leave you in completely different
        positions in two years. One has your customer list on it. The other has somebody else's, and
        you have been renting the audience the whole time without anybody calling it rent.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        I sell none of these and I take no cut of anything you sell. That is the only reason this
        page is worth reading rather than the comparisons you will find, which were mostly written by
        one of the companies on it.
      </p>
  </Section>

  {/* ── What this sheet prices, and what it deliberately does not ──────────
      The reading measure and the shortest rhythm on the page, against the
      widest column directly above. It keeps canvas: this is a preamble to the
      arithmetic rather than a section of its own, and giving it a ground would
      have announced it as the second of three grounds before the reader had met
      the first thing worth stopping at. */}
  <Section width="popout" density="tight" class="border-b border-line">
      <p class="mono-label">Read this before the table</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink">
        This prices one thing: what the platform takes.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Its monthly fee, plus its own percentage of what you sell. Not what your card processor
        charges, because some of these bundle that in and some leave you to pick, and a total mixing
        the two compares a bundle against a piece of one. That is why no two store comparisons on the
        internet agree with each other.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        The card processing half is a whole sheet of its own. Every rate on it was read the same day
        as these, and the arithmetic there runs on your own numbers in exactly the same way.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        And the largest cost of running a store is not on either sheet, because nothing can compute
        it. It is your hours. Every card below says what that platform does to them, and the tables
        print your own figure beside the money rather than folding it in.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7">
        <a href="/websites/getting-paid/" class="btn btn-secondary">
          What it costs you to be paid <ArrowUpRight class="h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
      </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The arithmetic ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      The first ground on the page, a wider column than the preamble above it,
      and the standard rhythm back. Both tables already run to the edge of their
      own box and scroll inside it, so the extra width goes to the prose and the
      hours panel around them rather than to the tables themselves. */}
  <Section tone="surface" width="content">
      <p class="mono-label">The arithmetic</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
        Four numbers of yours, and the ranking changes.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        What goes through the store in a month, what an average order is, how many things you list,
        and roughly how many hours a month it takes you. Two worked examples follow so you can see
        the shape — put your own four numbers in their place and the sum is identical.
      </p>

      {tables.map(({ example, table, spread, hours }) => (
        <div class="mt-10">
          <p class="mono-label">{example.title}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
            Assumed, not measured: {example.label}. These are my numbers for the example, not a claim
            about what your business does.
          </p>

          {/* The table is the one element allowed past the body width, and it
              scrolls inside its own box so the page itself never does.
              verify-mobile.mjs measures element boxes against the viewport. */}
          <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line">
            <table class="w-full min-w-[38rem] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-line">
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">Where you sell</th>
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">A month</th>
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">A year</th>
                  <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">What that really is, as a share of sales</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {table.map((r) => (
                  <tr class={`border-b border-line/60 ${r.id === 'aiq' ? 'bg-accent/5' : ''}`}>
                    <td class="p-4 align-top">
                      <span class="font-semibold text-ink">{r.name}</span>
                      {r.planName ? <span class="mt-1 block text-xs text-ink-faint">{r.planName}</span> : null}
                    </td>
                    {r.cents === null && r.lowCents !== undefined ? (
                      <>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{usd(r.lowCents)} to {usd(r.highCents)}</td>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{usd(r.lowAnnualCents)} to {usd(r.highAnnualCents)}</td>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-sm text-ink-faint">{r.problem}</td>
                      </>
                    ) : r.cents === null ? (
                      <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-faint" colspan="3">{r.problem}</td>
                    ) : (
                      <>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{usd(r.cents)}</td>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{usd(r.annualCents)}</td>
                        <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{pct(r.takeHundredths)}</td>
                      </>
                    )}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
            Cheapest to dearest, with the ones that cannot be worked out kept at the bottom rather
            than dropped. The gap between the top and the bottom of what could be priced is
            {' '}{usd(spread.differenceCents)} a year on those numbers — and every figure above is
            what the platform takes, before your card processor takes anything.
          </p>

          {/* THE HOURS, BESIDE THE MONEY AND NEVER INSIDE IT. The refusal is the
              product here: converting them needs an hourly figure for somebody
              else's time, which is the one made-up number this page will not
              print. */}
          <div class="panel mt-5 max-w-3xl p-6">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">
              And {hours.hours} hours a month of your own time, which is {hours.annualHours} hours a year.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{hours.problem}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
              What is worth doing instead is reading the hours row on each card below. The difference
              between the easiest and the hardest of these to run is larger than the difference
              between the cheapest and the dearest, and it is the one nobody puts on a pricing page.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}

      <p class="mt-8 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
        What the sum assumes, stated plainly: today's advertised prices, the plan each platform's own
        rules put you on, and no card processing of any kind. Where a figure leaves something real
        out, the card for it says what and why. It is arithmetic on published figures rather than a
        forecast, and the moment it needs a forecast it stops belonging here.
      </p>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The cards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Back to canvas and out to the widest column on the page. This is the
      longest band on the sheet and a reader works through it a card at a time
      rather than scanning it, so the two-column grid gets the room the banded
      arithmetic above could not use. */}
  <Section width="feature">
    <p class="mono-label">One at a time</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
      The same {rows.length} questions, asked of every one of them.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      Grouped by who owns the customer when the sale is done, because that is the thing you cannot
      change your mind about later. Mine is on the list and gets the same treatment as the rest.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      {cards.map((c) => (
        <div class={`panel lift flex flex-col gap-4 p-7 ${c.isMine ? 'border-accent/40' : ''}`}>
          <div>
            <p class={`mono-label ${c.isMine ? 'text-cyan-deep' : ''}`}>{c.kind}</p>
            <h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">{c.name}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink-faint">{c.how}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-muted">{c.headline}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">{c.assumption}</p>
            {/* WHAT THE FIGURE LEAVES OUT, BESIDE THE FIGURE AND NOT A SCREEN
                BELOW IT. Same rule verify-website.mjs applies to a price and its
                qualifier: a caveat a reader never reaches is a caveat that was
                not made. */}
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
              <span class="font-semibold">What that figure leaves out:</span> {c.leavesOut}
            </p>
          </div>
          <dl class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-t border-line pt-4">
            {rows.map((r) => (
              <div>
                <dt class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">{r.label}</dt>
                <dd class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{c[r.key]}</dd>
              </div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The catalog table — the finding the sheet turns on ───────────────
      The deep ground, back inside the reading column, and the only band on the
      page given the taller rhythm. Three signals at once against the wide
      canvas grid above it, because this is the one band a reader must not
      scroll past: it decides whether the thing sold here can be built for them.

      deep and not instrument: the panel under the table is styled for a light
      ground, which is the reading /work/index.astro wrote down when it declined
      the dark chassis for its own flagship panel. */}
  <Section tone="deep" width="content" density="tall">
      <p class="mono-label">The one thing I sell here</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
        Whether a page I build can read your catalog depends entirely on which platform you are on.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        The thing worth building here is a page on your own site where a customer configures what
        they actually want — a size, a finish, a piece of text, whatever your work varies by — and
        then lands in your store's own checkout with it. You add a listing in your store and the page
        follows, because it reads your catalog live rather than holding a copy I have to update.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        {canBuild.length} of the {catalogAccess.length} platforms below let an outside page do that with a credential that
        is safe in a browser. {cannotBuild.length} do not, and the reason is almost always the same: they require a
        secret on every request, which would mean me running a server holding a key that can read
        your orders. That is your customers' data and I do not hold it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">This is not a ranking and a "no" is not a mark against a platform.</span>{' '}
        It is a fact about what I can build, not about how good they are at selling. Several of the
        ones that refuse are the best products on this page.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[44rem] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line">
              <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">Platform</th>
              <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">Can a page I build read it</th>
              <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">What it needs</th>
              <th class="p-4 font-display font-semibold text-ink">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {catalogAccess.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-line/60">
                <td class="p-4 align-top font-semibold text-ink">{r.platform}</td>
                <td class="p-4 align-top">
                  <span class={r.verdict === 'no' ? 'text-ink-faint' : 'font-semibold text-cyan-deep'}>
                    {VERDICTS[r.verdict].label}
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{r.credential}</td>
                <td class="p-4 align-top text-ink-muted">{r.detail}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="panel mt-8 max-w-3xl p-7">
        <p class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-ink">
          There is a second version of this, and it is worse for most people.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
          A configurator can also hold its own copy of your catalog instead of reading your store's.
          That version works on any platform, including all the ones above that say no — and it means
          every new thing you list, I have to add. If you want to add your own listings and have the
          page keep up, it has to read your store, and that is what the table answers.
        </p>
      </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The shared capability list ────────────────────────────────────────
      SHARED WITH /websites/compare/ AND /getting-paid/, out of
      src/lib/capabilities.js. It sits after the catalog table deliberately: a
      reader who has just been told what can be built on their platform is the
      one who should meet what will not be built on any of them. */}
  <WhatIBuild sheet="stores" />

  {/* ── What this is not ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Canvas and the reading measure, against the shared band's surface and its
      wider column. The standard rhythm rather than the tight one the preamble
      near the top uses: this is four paragraphs and a closing ask where that
      was a single short block, and squeezing the longest prose on the page is
      how a band ends up looking cramped rather than deliberate. */}
  <Section width="popout">
    <p class="mono-label">What this is not</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink">
      Not a recommendation, and not a claim that any of these are bad.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      They are well built and they are run by companies with more engineers than this town has
      people. Nothing here argues otherwise. What the sheet does is put the fee beside the question
      nobody asks, which is what happens to your customer list if you ever want to leave.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      It is also not complete, and two gaps are worth naming rather than hiding. TikTok Shop's
      referral fee sits behind a seller login that would not open, so it is not printed. And the
      commerce plan prices for Wix and Squarespace were not read on this pass — their website plans
      were, on the other sheet, and the plan that sells is a different one.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      It is not tax advice either, and the line matters. Who owes sales tax on a sale depends on who
      the legal seller is, which differs between a marketplace and your own store. What nobody here
      will ever do is work out whether you owe any. That is a question for your accountant.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      And it is not a list I keep current by remembering to. Every figure above is registered with
      the date it was read, it expires on its own, and this page carries that date in plain sight.
      One line on this site went stale exactly that way and the register is what caught it.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 border-t border-line pt-10">
      <p class="mono-label">If you want the sum done on your own numbers</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink">
        Send me the four numbers and I will send the table back.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        A month of store sales, an average order, how many things you list, and roughly how many
        hours a month it takes you. No charge and no meeting. Tell me which platform you are on and
        the catalog answer comes back with it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a href="/contact/?intent=question" class="btn btn-primary">
          Send me the four numbers <ArrowUpRight class="h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
        <a href="/websites/getting-paid/" class="btn btn-secondary">The same reading, for card processing</a>
        <a href="/websites/compare/" class="btn btn-secondary">And for websites</a>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
        Every fee and every term on this page was read off those companies' own pages on
        {' '}{READ_ON}. They change them without announcing it, so if you are weighing this up, go
        and read them again yourself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</MarketingLayout>
